3. Facts are as follows: The appellant, a Government Contractor was given the contract for premixed bituminous concrete work on the Air Port Road, Dacca. One of the terms and condition of the tender was that the material required for the work would be supplied to the contractor who will manufacture those at the Ashphalt plant of the Government at Kathal bagan and carry the manufactured materials to the work site and the work was to be done by the Operators, Technicians and Engineers of the Government and the fuel and hire-charges for the machinery were payable by the contractor by deduction from his bills. After the completion of the work certain materials were left over at the site and the appellant contractor returned the same to the Executive Engineer. At the time of the preparation of the bills the Executive Engineer decided to deduct the cost of the materials returned by the contractor as per clause 8 of the additional terms and conditions of the contract. This was disputed by the Contractor and according to the terms of the contract the matter was referred to the Arbitrator. The Superintending Engineer (R &amp; H) made his award as Arbitrator on 30-10-78. This award was filed to the Court whereupon the Contractor filed an application under section 33 read with section 30 of the Arbitration Act for setting aside the award. This case was registered as Miscellaneous Case No. 1 of 1979. The learned Subordinate Judge after hearing the parties disallowed the miscellaneous case. The matter came before the High Court Division as F.M.A No. 494 of 1980. The High Court Division considered two points namely (1) whether the costs of the materials not consumed but returned by the appellant contractor were deductible under clause 8 of the additional terms and conditions of the contract and secondly whether the supplementary tender items given by the Contractor were admissible. The High Court Division allowed the appeal in part by deciding the first point against the appellant and on the second point, namely, the admissibility of the supplementary tender items, the award was directed to be remitted to the Arbitrator for reconsideration. 

 5. The High Court Division also considered the clause and took the view that even if the clause would be interpreted differently the Court will not interfere with the award unless it can be shown that award is within the mischief of section 33 of the Arbitration Act. Reliance was placed on the case of Government of Kelantan V. Duff Development Company Ltd. 1923, A.C. 395.&nbsp; 

 6. Mr. Sharif has submitted that since the unconsumed materials benefited the Government there was no scope for applying clause 8 in This case. This contention cannot be accepted. 

 10. Let us take a case where the above contractor finished the said 100 cft. of brick work with 1000 Nos. of bricks (in place of 1100 Nos) and 3 bags of cement (in place of 4 bags of cement). It is possible due to bigger size of bricks, thick mortar used and using less cement in the mortar. So he has consumed 100 nos. of bricks and one bag of cement less than the specified.&nbsp; 

 13. Thus the factual position is that when the contract is given on issue rate the Contractor is required to do the job as per specification and the consumption of less materials would bring him within the mischief of clause 8. To hold otherwise is to give the benefit to the contractor for the work done with less materials and to be paid for a rate which he did not quote nor was this his issue rate.&nbsp; 

 14. Mr. Sharif had no answer to this practical proposition which was submitted by the respondent. In clause 8 there is a compulsive force. The materials required for the purpose as per specification must be used and less consumption of materials has been discouraged. In the given instance, as cited by the respondents, the contractor would reap the benefit of wages of a work which was not performed as he used less materials and consequently less labour. To allow payment in such case would mean a premium for a work not performed. To for stall such claim the clause 8 was inserted and the emphasis was given for proper completion of the contract work and less consumption of materials was discouraged by insertion of the condition &quot;even not issued to the contractor physically as penal action&quot;. The appellant accepted such term in the contract. He cannot now resale his stand. There the matter ends. However, we are informed by the Counsel for the appellant that this clause has been deleted from the contract form.&nbsp; 

 15. The dispute is over a contract and the Arbitrator is the sole judge. Unless the award of the Arbitrator can be assailed on one of the three grounds mentioned in section 33 of the Arbitration Act, the long line of cases show that the Court will not interfere even though courts may take a different view of the interpretation of the particular terms of contract. This is a sound principle. Otherwise such disputes should be brought within the jurisdiction of the civil court and thereby opening a flood-gate of litigations nullifying the very spirit of the Arbitration as mentioned in the Arbitration Act. Reliance may be placed on the decision of Govt. of Kelatan V. Duff Development Company, 1923 A.C. 395. It was held where a question of construction is specifically referred to Arbitration the decision of the arbitrator on that point cannot be set aside because the court could have come to a different conclusion unless it could be shown that the arbitrator has proceeded illegally e.g. that he has decided on inadmissible evidence or on principles of construction which the law does not countenance. No such case has been made out here.&nbsp;
